QB.add(17,[{"n":1,"q":"A 45-year-old woman presents with a temperature of 105°F, heart rate of 160 bpm, and altered mental status, including agitation and anxiety following an abrupt discontinuation of her antithyroid medication. Upon examination, she is diaphoretic, has a goitre, and exhibits hand tremors. Which of the following treatment strategies is most appropriate for managing her condition?","o":[{"t":"Start with methimazole, followed by an iodine solution after 2 hours."},{"t":"Start propranolol and administer hydrocortisone."},{"t":"Initiate IV fluids and oxygen and start with cholestyramine."},{"t":"Perform a thyroidectomy immediately without any pre-treatment measures."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient is diagnosed with thyroid storm, an acute and life-threatening exacerbation of hyperthyroidism characterized by high fever, severe tachycardia, diaphoresis, hand tremors, and altered mental status. The abrupt discontinuation of her antithyroid medication likely triggered this crisis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hydrocortisone (100 mg IV every 8 hours) and propranolol (40-80 mg every 4-6 hours) are essential in managing the symptoms and controlling adrenergic tone."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thyroid Storm"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"About A thyroid storm, or thyrotoxic crisis, is an acute,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"life-threatening complication of hyperthyroidism. It presents with multisystem involvement and has a mortality rate of 8 to 25% despite advancements in treatment."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Etiology Most commonly associated with Graves' disease but"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"can also occur in toxic multinodular goitre and toxic thyroid adenoma. Precipitating Factors: Abrupt discontinuation of antithyroid medications. Thyroid surgery. Non-thyroid surgery. Acute illnesses (e.g., infections, diabetic ketoacidosis, myocardial infarction, stroke) act as stressors that can trigger excess thyroid hormone release. Parturition (childbirth). Recent use of iodinated contrast medium. Trauma or Burns or traumatic brain injury. Medication side effects (e.g., amiodarone, anesthetics, salicylates). Raised beta-hcg conditions such as complete mole, twin pregnancies"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Fever: High temperature (104°F to 106°F) with Features diaphoresis."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Cardiovascular Involvement: Tachycardia (>140 bpm), heart failure, arrhythmias, hypotension, and risk of cardiac arrest. CNS Manifestations: Agitation, delirium, anxiety, psychosis, or coma. Gastrointestinal Symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain, intestinal obstruction, and acute hepatic failure. Physical Exam Findings: Tachycardia, goitre, hand tremors, moist and warm skin, hyperreflexia, systolic hypertension, and jaundice."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thyroid Storm"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management Initial Supportive Measures: IV fluids, oxygen, cooling"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"blankets, and acetaminophen. Beta-blocker Therapy: Start with propranolol immediately (40-80 mg every 4-6 hours) to control adrenergic tone. Thionamide Therapy: Administer propylthiouracil (500-1000 mg loading, then 250 mg every 4 hours) or methimazole (20 mg every 4-6 hours). This reduces thyroid hormone synthesis. Iodine Solution: Give SSKI (5 drops every 6 hours) after thionamide to reduce the release of thyroid hormone Glucocorticoids: Hydrocortisone (100 mg IV every 8 hours) or dexamethasone (2 mg every 6 hours). Therapy to block peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 : Iodinated radiocontrast agent, glucocorticoid, PTU, propranolol Cholestyramine (Bile acid sequestrant) : 4 grams orally 4 times daily for severe cases to reduce enterohepatic recycling of thyroid hormone. Monitoring: Admit to ICU with close cardiac monitoring and ventilatory support if needed. Alternative Medications: Esmolol as a short-acting beta-blocker; diltiazem if beta-blockers are contraindicated. Surgery: Thyroidectomy for patients with contraindications to thionamides after pre-treatment. Definitive Treatment: Recommend radioiodine therapy or surgery for hyperthyroidism once stabilized."},{"k":"img","v":5178},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Start with methimazole followed by iodine solution after 2 hours. (Option A):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta-blockers are given initially to prevent stress on the heart and manage sympathetic symptoms. Propranolol is the DOC for thyroid storm."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"After this, we start the patient on PTU > methimazole and after a few hours, we add SSKI."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Iodine should not be given before thionamides (such as methimazole or propylthiouracil) as it can increase thyroid hormone synthesis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Initiate IV fluids and oxygen, and start with cholestyramine. (Option C): While supportive care is important, cholestyramine is not the first-line treatment for acute management."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Perform a thyroidectomy immediately without any pre-treatment measures. (Option D): Surgery requires pre-treatment with beta-blockers and glucocorticoids for stabilization."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 32-year-old woman with newly diagnosed Graves disease is scheduled for thyroidectomy. Her current thyroid function tests show: TSH: <0.01 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0) Free T4: 45 pmol/L (normal 10-20) Free T3: 15 pmol/L (normal 3.5-6.5) Which of the following is the most appropriate preoperative management to prevent thyroid storm?","o":[{"t":"Start propranolol alone one day before surgery"},{"t":"Start methimazole and wait for normal thyroid function tests"},{"t":"Start methimazole, potassium iodide, and propranolol 7-10 days before surgery"},{"t":"D) Proceed directly to surgery with close monitoring"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Proper preoperative preparation is essential to prevent thyroid storm in patients with uncontrolled hyperthyroidism undergoing thyroidectomy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The appropriate preoperative management involves:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blocks new thyroid hormone synthesis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Should be started first to reduce thyroid hormone levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduces thyroid vascularity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blocks hormone release"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Must be given after thionamides to prevent increased hormone synthesis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Controls sympathetic symptoms"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blocks peripheral conversion of T4 to T3"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thyroid storm is characterized by:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The Burch-Wartofsky scoring system is used to help diagnose thyroid storm:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Burch-Wartofsky scoring system"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criteria Points"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Temperature (°C)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criteria Points"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"37.2–37.7 5 37.8–38.2 10 38.3–38.8 15 38.9–39.2 20 39.3–39.9 25 ‡40.0 30"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Central Nervous System Effects"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Absent 0 Mild (agitation) 10 Moderate (delirium, psychosis, extreme lethargy) 20 Severe (seizures, coma) 30"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Gastrointestinal-Hepatic Dysfunction"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Absent 0 Moderate (diarrhea, nausea/vomiting, abdominal pain) 10 Severe (unexplained jaundice) 20"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cardiovascular Dysfunction- Tachycardia (beats/min)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"90–109 5 110–119 10 120–129 15 130–139 20 ‡140 25 Atrial fibrillation 10 Heart failure 5–15"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Precipitant History 10"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Interpretation of Burch-Wartofsky score: ‡45 points: Highly suggestive of thyroid storm 25-44 points: Impending thyroid storm <25 points: Unlikely to represent thyroid storm"}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 45-year-old woman with a history of multinodular goitre undergoes a CT angiogram with iodinated contrast for suspected pulmonary embolism. Two weeks later, she presents with palpitations, heat intolerance, and weight loss. Her thyroid function tests show suppressed TSH and elevated T3 and T4. Which of the following best explains this patient's current condition?","o":[{"t":"Wolff-Chaikoff effect"},{"t":"Jod-Basedow effect"},{"t":"Thyroid storm"},{"t":"Hashitoxicosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient shows features of hyperthyroidism, which occurred after receiving iodinated contrast during a CT angiogram. This Iodine-induced hyperthyroidism is called the Jod-Basedow effect, which occurs due to increased iodine trapping leading to Increased T4, T3 production."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wolff Chaikoff effect & Jod Basedow effect"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Wolff-Chaikoff Effect Jod-Basedow Effect"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition Temporary inhibition of thyroid Iodine-induced hyperthyroidism"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"hormone synthesis in response to high iodine exposure"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mechanism High iodine levels inhibit thyroid Excess iodine stimulates"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"peroxidase, reducing T3 and T4 autonomous thyroid hormone production production in susceptible individuals"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Duration Temporary (usually resolves in 1-2 Can persist until treated"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"weeks)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thyroid Initially decreased T3 and T4, Increased T3 and T4, decreased Function increased TSH TSH Tests"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"At-risk Normal thyroid glands Pre-existing thyroid disease (e.g., Population multinodular goiter, Graves' disease)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Usually asymptomatic or mild Symptoms of hyperthyroidism (e.g., Presentatio hypothyroid symptoms palpitations, heat intolerance, n weight loss)"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 52-year-old woman presents with a 2-week history of palpitations, anxiety, and a 4 kg weight loss. She has no significant past medical history and takes no medications. On examination, her heart rate is 110 bpm and regular, and she has a small, painful goitre. Thyroid function tests show: TSH: 0.02 mIU/L (normal range: 0.4-4.0 mIU/L) Free T4: 28 pmol/L (normal range: 10-20 pmol/L) Free T3: 6.2 pmol/L (normal range: 3.5-6.5 pmol/L) A thyroid scan shows very low uptake. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Graves' disease"},{"t":"Toxic multinodular goiter"},{"t":"Subacute thyroiditis"},{"t":"TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Here thyroid scan / RAIU scan shows very low uptake, indicating thyroid gland is not active, but free T4 and T3 are elevated due to inflammation of the thyroid gland indicating thyroiditis as a cause of this patient's condition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subacute thyroiditis (de Quervain thyroiditis) is an immune reaction of thyroid gland (tender thyroid gland ) that follows an upper respiratory tract infection (viral infections)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's features are indicative of thyrotoxicosis without hyperthyroidism , a condition where thyroid hormone levels are elevated but the thyroid gland is not overactive."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyrotoxicosis: A clinical syndrome that occurs when there is an excess of circulating thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) in the bloodstream, regardless of the source."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperthyroidism: A specific condition where the thyroid gland itself is overactive and produces an excessive amount of thyroid hormones."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"From the given options, Subacute thyroiditis is one of the causes for thyrotoxicosis without hyperthyroidism, in which the clinical thyrotoxicosis symptoms result from the release of thyroid hormone from the inflamed tissue during the thyrotoxic phase."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"During the thyrotoxic phase of Subacute thyrotoxicosis , there is a transient release of stored thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) into the bloodstream due to the inflammation of thyroid tissue."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This results in the symptoms of thyrotoxicosis (e.g., palpitations, weight loss, heat intolerance, etc), but it is not caused by increased synthesis of thyroid hormone, as seen in hyperthyroidism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The thyrotoxic phase usually lasts for several weeks, followed by a hypothyroid phase and eventual recovery."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of thyrotoxicosis without hyperthyroidism include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subacute thyroiditis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Silent thyroiditis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Factitious thyrotoxicosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation/Amiodarone/Infarction of large tumor"},{"k":"img","v":5185},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The other options (Options A, B, D) cause thyrotoxicosis due to hyperthyroidism, meaning the thyroid gland is actively overproducing thyroid hormones (high uptake on RAIU scan)."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 35-year-old woman presents with a 2-month history of weight loss, palpitations, and heat intolerance. On examination, she has a diffuse goitre, lid lag, and fine tremors in her outstretched hands. Her thyroid function tests show: TSH: < 0.01 mIU/L (normal range: 0.4-4.0 mIU/L) Free T4: 35 pmol/L (normal range: 10-20 pmol/L) Free T3: 12 pmol/L (normal range: 3.5-6.5 pmol/L) Which of the following is the most likely cause of her hyperthyroidism?","o":[{"t":"Toxic multinodular goiter"},{"t":"Graves' disease"},{"t":"TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma"},{"t":"Iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient's presentation is most consistent with Graves' disease, which is the most common cause of primary hyperthyroidism . The diagnosis is supported by:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Primary and Secondary Hyperthyroidism"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cause Key Features for identification"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Primary Hyperthyroidism (pathology within the thyroid gland)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Graves' disease Diffuse goitre, ophthalmopathy, TSH receptor antibodies"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(LATS- long-acting thyroid stimulating antibodies)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Toxic multinodular goiter Multiple autonomous nodules, older patients"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Toxic adenoma Single autonomous nodule"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Subacute thyroiditis (de Quervain Painful thyroid, recent viral illness, transient thyroiditis)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Silent thyroiditis (Painless lymphocytic Painless, often postpartum, transient thyroiditis)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis History of iodine exposure (e.g., contrast, amiodarone)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Drug-induced (e.g., amiodarone) History of relevant medication use"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secondary Hyperthyroidism"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma Inappropriate TSH secretion, other pituitary hormones"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"may be affected"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"hCG-mediated hyperthyroidism Pregnancy, gestational trophoblastic disease"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Alpha subunit of hCG is similar in structure to TSH)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thyroid hormone resistance (rare) Genetic disorder, reduced sensitivity of the body to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"thyroid hormone."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ectopic Hyperthyroidism"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Struma ovarii Ovarian teratoma with functional thyroid tissue"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Toxic multinodular goiter (Option A): Typically occurs in older individuals with a nodular goiter, not a diffuse one like this patient."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma (Option C): Would show elevated or normal TSH, whereas this patient has suppressed TSH."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Iodine-induced thyrotoxicosis (Option D): Requires recent iodine exposure, which is not mentioned in this case."},{"k":"img","v":5192}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 50-year-old man presents with complaints of irritability, difficulty sleeping, and unexplained weight loss of 10 kg over the past 3 months despite an increased appetite. On examination, his heart rate is 98 bpm, and he has a fine tremor in his hands. His wife mentions that his eyes seem more prominent lately. Which of the following additional findings would be least likely in this patient if he has hyperthyroidism?","o":[{"t":"Warm, moist palms"},{"t":"Proximal muscle weakness"},{"t":"Delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes"},{"t":"Frequent bowel movements"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes is characteristic of hypothyroidism, not hyperthyroidism . In hyperthyroidism, clinical finding typically seen is hyperreflexia or increased deep tendon reflexes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Hyperthyroidism"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"System Signs and Symptoms Pathogenesis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"General - Weight loss despite - Increased basal metabolic rate"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"increased appetite and thermogenesis - Heat intolerance - Enhanced catabolism and protein - Increased sweating breakdown - Fatigue - Increased sensitivity to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"catecholamines - Hypermetabolic state with increased ATP consumption"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"System Signs and Symptoms Pathogenesis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cardiovascular - Tachycardia - Increased b-adrenergic receptor"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Palpitations sensitivity - Widened pulse pressure - Direct effect of T3 on cardiac - Atrial fibrillation myocytes"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Reduced peripheral vascular resistance - Enhanced automaticity of cardiac tissue"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neurological - Fine tremor - Enhanced sympathetic response"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Hyperreflexia - Increased speed of muscle - Anxiety, irritability contraction and relaxation - Insomnia - Increased sensitivity of CNS to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"catecholamines - Accelerated cerebral metabolic rate"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Gastrointestinal - Increased bowel - Increased gut motility"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"movements - Enhanced sympathetic activity - Diarrhea - Accelerated intestinal transit time - Malabsorption - Increased secretory activity"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dermatological - Warm, moist skin - Peripheral vasodilation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Fine, thin hair - Increased cutaneous blood flow - Onycholysis - Enhanced catabolism affecting"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"hair/nail growth - Increased skin temperature"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ocular (Graves' - Exophthalmos - Autoimmune inflammation of specific) - Lid lag orbital tissues"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Lid retraction - Increased sympathetic tone to - Periorbital edema levator muscles"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Glycosaminoglycan deposition in orbital tissues - Lymphocytic infiltration of orbital muscles"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Musculoskeletal - Proximal muscle - Thyroid hormone stimulates"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"weakness osteoclasts - Osteoporosis - Enhanced protein catabolism - Muscle wasting - Increased bone turnover with net"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"resorption (as thyroid hormone stimulates of Osteoclasts) - Direct effect of excess thyroid hormone on muscle metabolism"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Reproductive - Menstrual irregularities - Altered sex hormone-binding"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Decreased fertility globulin levels - Gynecomastia (in men) - Changes in GnRH pulsatility"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Increased aromatization of androgens to estrogens"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"System Signs and Symptoms Pathogenesis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Thyroid Storm - High fever - Massive release of thyroid"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Severe tachycardia/arrhyt hormones hmias - Extreme hypermetabolic state - Delirium/coma - Enhanced tissue response to - Multi-organ failure catecholamines"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Cytokine storm and systemic inflammatory response"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Warm, moist palms (Option A): This is common in hyperthyroidism due to increased metabolism and sweating."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Proximal muscle weakness (Option B): Hyperthyroidism often causes muscle weakness, especially in the proximal muscles."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Frequent bowel movements (Option D): Hyperthyroidism increases gut motility, leading to more frequent bowel movements."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with unexplained weight loss, anxiety, and palpitations for the past 2 months. On examination, his heart rate is 100 bpm, and he has a fine tremor in his hands. Initial thyroid function tests show: TSH: 0.02 mIU/L (normal range: 0.4-4.0 mIU/L) Free T4: 1.8 ng/dL (normal range: 0.8-1.8 ng/dL) Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in the workup?","o":[{"t":"Measure Free T3 levels"},{"t":"Perform a radioactive iodine uptake scan"},{"t":"Check TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb)"},{"t":"Perform thyroid ultrasound"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's clinical presentation suggests hyperthyroidism , which is supported by the suppressed TSH. However, the Free T4 is normal."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, measuring Free T3 is the most appropriate next step to confirm the diagnosis of T3 thyrotoxicosis, where T3 levels are elevated but T4 may be normal or only slightly elevated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T3 thyrotoxicosis occurs due to increased peripheral Deiodinase enzyme activity, which converts free T4 to free T3."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thyroid Function Test Workup:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initial Screening: TSH (Thyroid Stimulating Hormone) Normal range: 0.4-4.0 mIU/L"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal range: 0.4-4.0 mIU/L"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follow-up Tests (if TSH is abnormal): a) Free T4 (thyroxine) b) Free T3 (triiodothyronine) if T4 is normal but TSH is suppressed c) Consider total T4 and T3 tests, but free hormone tests are preferred"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"a) Free T4 (thyroxine)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"b) Free T3 (triiodothyronine) if T4 is normal but TSH is suppressed"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"c) Consider total T4 and T3 tests, but free hormone tests are preferred"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Interpretation of Results:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal range: 0.4-4.0 mIU/L"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"a) Free T4 (thyroxine)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"b) Free T3 (triiodothyronine) if T4 is normal but TSH is suppressed"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"c) Consider total T4 and T3 tests, but free hormone tests are preferred"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Condition TSH Free T4 Free T3"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Primary hypothyroidism › fl fl"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Primary hyperthyroidism fl (<0.1 mIU/L) › ›"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Subclinical hypothyroidism › Normal Normal"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Subclinical hyperthyroidis fl Normal Normal m"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Secondary hypothyroidism fl or Approximately normal fl fl"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"T3 thyrotoxicosis fl Normal ›"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Additional Tests for Etiology: a) Anti-TPO (thyroid peroxidase) antibodies Positive in 5-15% of euthyroid women, up to 2% of euthyroid men Present in almost all autoimmune hypothyroidism cases Present in up to 80% of Graves' disease cases b) TRAb (TSH receptor antibody) or TSI (Thyroid Stimulating Immunoglobulin) (Option C) For Graves' disease diagnosis Used to predict remission after antithyroid drug treatment Important in pregnancy to assess risk of fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis c) Thyroglobulin (Tg) Elevated in all types of thyrotoxicosis except factitia Main use: follow-up of thyroid cancer patients post-treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"a) Anti-TPO (thyroid peroxidase) antibodies Positive in 5-15% of euthyroid women, up to 2% of euthyroid men Present in almost all autoimmune hypothyroidism cases Present in up to 80% of Graves' disease cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive in 5-15% of euthyroid women, up to 2% of euthyroid men"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Present in almost all autoimmune hypothyroidism cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Present in up to 80% of Graves' disease cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"b) TRAb (TSH receptor antibody) or TSI (Thyroid Stimulating Immunoglobulin) (Option C) For Graves' disease diagnosis Used to predict remission after antithyroid drug treatment Important in pregnancy to assess risk of fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For Graves' disease diagnosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Used to predict remission after antithyroid drug treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Important in pregnancy to assess risk of fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"c) Thyroglobulin (Tg) Elevated in all types of thyrotoxicosis except factitia Main use: follow-up of thyroid cancer patients post-treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated in all types of thyrotoxicosis except factitia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Main use: follow-up of thyroid cancer patients post-treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Imaging Studies:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"a) Anti-TPO (thyroid peroxidase) antibodies Positive in 5-15% of euthyroid women, up to 2% of euthyroid men Present in almost all autoimmune hypothyroidism cases Present in up to 80% of Graves' disease cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive in 5-15% of euthyroid women, up to 2% of euthyroid men"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Present in almost all autoimmune hypothyroidism cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Present in up to 80% of Graves' disease cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"b) TRAb (TSH receptor antibody) or TSI (Thyroid Stimulating Immunoglobulin) (Option C) For Graves' disease diagnosis Used to predict remission after antithyroid drug treatment Important in pregnancy to assess risk of fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For Graves' disease diagnosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Used to predict remission after antithyroid drug treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Important in pregnancy to assess risk of fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"c) Thyroglobulin (Tg) Elevated in all types of thyrotoxicosis except factitia Main use: follow-up of thyroid cancer patients post-treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated in all types of thyrotoxicosis except factitia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Main use: follow-up of thyroid cancer patients post-treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive in 5-15% of euthyroid women, up to 2% of euthyroid men"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Present in almost all autoimmune hypothyroidism cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Present in up to 80% of Graves' disease cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For Graves' disease diagnosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Used to predict remission after antithyroid drug treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Important in pregnancy to assess risk of fetal/neonatal thyrotoxicosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated in all types of thyrotoxicosis except factitia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Main use: follow-up of thyroid cancer patients post-treatment"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indication: All patients with suspected thyroid nodules"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Evaluates nodule characteristics (size, composition, echogenicity, margins, calcifications)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fine Needle Aspiration (FNA) Biopsy: Indicated for suspicious nodules based on ultrasound criteria Ultrasound-guided FNA preferred for improved accuracy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indicated for suspicious nodules based on ultrasound criteria"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ultrasound-guided FNA preferred for improved accuracy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indicated for suspicious nodules based on ultrasound criteria"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ultrasound-guided FNA preferred for improved accuracy"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Radioactive iodine uptake scan (Option B): While useful in differentiating causes of hyperthyroidism (e.g., Graves' disease vs. thyroiditis), it is not the immediate next best step. It is the next step after TFT shows a hyperthyroid picture."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Graves' disease: Enlarged gland, homogeneous increased uptake"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Toxic adenoma: Focal area of increased uptake, suppressed remainder"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Toxic multinodular goitre: Multiple areas of increased/decreased uptake"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyroiditis: Very low uptake"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyrotoxicosis factitia: Low uptake"}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 35-year-old female presents with palpitations, heat intolerance, and weight loss despite increased appetite over the past 3 months. On examination, she has a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland. A thyroid scan is performed, shown below: What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Toxic multinodular goiter"},{"t":"Graves' disease"},{"t":"Thyroiditis"},{"t":"Thyroid adenoma"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation of hyperthyroidism symptoms (palpitations, heat intolerance, weight loss with increased appetite) along with a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland is highly suggestive of Graves' disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In Graves' disease, there is diffusely increased uptake throughout an enlarged gland , often described as a \" hot \" appearance."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thyroid scan:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyroid scans use radioactive iodine (I-123) or technetium-99m to assess thyroid function and structure on RAIU scans."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased uptake indicates hyperfunction, while decreased uptake suggests hypofunction or thyroiditis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The pattern of uptake helps differentiate between diffuse (Graves') and nodular (toxic adenoma, multinodular) causes of hyperthyroidism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyroid scans are useful when TSH is suppressed to determine the cause of hyperthyroidism."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Interpretation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal Thyroid : Uniform uptake throughout the gland."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Graves' Disease : Diffusely increased uptake throughout an enlarged gland."},{"k":"img","v":5200},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fig. Thyroid scan in Grave’s disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Toxic Multinodular Goiter : Multiple areas of increased and decreased uptake. (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fig. Thyroid scan in Toxic multinodular goitre"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Toxic Adenoma : Single area of increased uptake with suppression of surrounding tissue. (Option D)"},{"k":"img","v":5202},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fig. Thyroid scan in Toxic Adenoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyroiditis : Very low or no uptake due to inflammation and destruction of thyroid tissue. (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fig. Thyroid scan-Normal vs Thyroiditis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyroid Cancer : Usually appears as \"cold\" nodules (areas of decreased uptake)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fig. Thyroid scan in Thyroid cancer"}],"qi":[5165]},{"n":9,"q":"A 32-year-old woman presents with palpitations, weight loss, and heat intolerance for the past 3 months. On examination, she has a diffuse goitre and mild proptosis. Thyroid function tests show suppressed TSH and elevated free T4 and T3. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?","o":[{"t":"Radioiodine ablation"},{"t":"B. Total thyroidectomy"},{"t":"Start propranolol and carbimazole"},{"t":"D. Thyroid ultrasonography"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation and thyroid function tests are consistent with Graves' disease. The most appropriate initial management is to start antithyroid medication (carbimazole) along with a beta-blocker (propranolol) to control symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Graves' disease is an autoimmune disorder characterised by thyrotoxicosis, diffuse goitre, and ophthalmopathy. Diagnosis is based on clinical features and thyroid function tests showing suppressed TSH with elevated free T4 and/or T3."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thyroid ultrasonography (Option D): While ultrasound can be useful in evaluating thyroid nodules or measuring thyroid volume, the diagnosis of hyperthyroidism and Graves' disease is clear from the clinical presentation and thyroid function tests. Imaging is not necessary at this stage. Instead, immediate management with antithyroid medications and symptomatic treatment is the appropriate next step."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secondary Hyperthyroidism:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Secondary hyperthyroidism refers to excessive thyroid hormone production due to extrathyroidal causes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated free T4 and/or T3 levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inappropriately normal or elevated TSH levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI of the pituitary to identify adenoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes with Treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"First-line: Transsphenoidal surgery to remove the adenoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If surgery is contraindicated or unsuccessful: Somatostatin analogs (e.g., octreotide, lanreotide) to suppress TSH secretion Dopamine agonists (e.g., cabergoline) may be effective in some cases Radiation therapy for residual tumor"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin analogs (e.g., octreotide, lanreotide) to suppress TSH secretion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dopamine agonists (e.g., cabergoline) may be effective in some cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation therapy for residual tumor"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Somatostatin analogs (e.g., octreotide, lanreotide) to suppress TSH secretion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dopamine agonists (e.g., cabergoline) may be effective in some cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Radiation therapy for residual tumor"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta-blockers to manage symptoms"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment to manage any associated pituitary abnormalities"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment of the underlying tumor (e.g., choriocarcinoma, hydatidiform mole)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In all cases, beta-blockers may be used to manage symptoms of thyrotoxicosis until definitive treatment takes effect."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antithyroid drugs are generally not effective in secondary hyperthyroidism as they do not address the underlying cause of excessive TSH production."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A newborn presents with prolonged jaundice, feeding problems, and hypotonia. The mother has a history of Hashimoto's thyroiditis. Neonatal screening reveals elevated TSH and low T4 levels. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the infant's condition?","o":[{"t":"A) Thyroid gland dysgenesis"},{"t":"Inborn error of thyroid hormone synthesis"},{"t":"Transplacental passage of TSH receptor-blocking antibodies"},{"t":"D) Iodine deficiency"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Transplacental passage of TSH receptor-blocking antibodies is the most likely cause, given the mother's history of Hashimoto's thyroiditis. TSH receptor-blocking antibodies can cross the placenta and cause transient congenital hypothyroidism in about 5% of affected newborns."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Congenital hypothyroidism:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Present at birth due to thyroid gland abnormalities or defects in hormone synthesis. Occurs in about 1 in 2000-4000 newborns."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The most common causes are:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lab Values:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TSH - › in Primary Congenital hypothyroidism Normal/fl in Secondary Congenital hypothyroidism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"› in Primary Congenital hypothyroidism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal/fl in Secondary Congenital hypothyroidism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Free T3/T4 - fl"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"› in Primary Congenital hypothyroidism"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal/fl in Secondary Congenital hypothyroidism"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prolonged jaundice"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotonia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Enlarged tongue"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Umbilical hernia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Developmental delay if untreated"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment: Levothyroxine (T4) replacement. Early treatment is critical to prevent neurodevelopmental delay."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Primary vs Secondary Hypothyroidism:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Primary Hypothyroidism Secondary Hypothyroidism"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition Thyroid gland dysfunction leads to Pituitary dysfunction leads to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"inadequate thyroid hormone insufficient TSH secretion production"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Causes Autoimmune (Hashimoto's Pituitary tumors"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"thyroiditis) Pituitary surgery or radiation Iatrogenic (e.g., post-thyroidectomy, Sheehan’s syndrome radiation) Trauma Iodine deficiency Genetic pituitary hormone Medications (e.g., amiodarone, deficiencies lithium) Non-obstetric cause of pituitary Infiltrative diseases (e.g., damage called Simmonds's disease sarcoidosis) (AVM, tumor, HTN) Food goitrogen fi Cabbage, Sarcoidosis cassava. Hemochromatosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Lab TSH - ›; TSH - Normal or fl;"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Free T3/T4 - fl Free T3/T4 - fl"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Fatigue, weight gain, cold Similar to primary hypothyroidism Features intolerance Other pituitary hormone deficiencies"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Dry skin, hair loss (e.g., low cortisol, low LH/FSH) Bradycardia, constipation Myxedema (puffy face, non-pitting edema)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatme Levothyroxine (T4) replacement Levothyroxine (T4) replacement, but nt with dosage based on TSH levels monitored by free T4 levels (since"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"TSH is unreliable)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thyroid gland dysgenesis (Option A) and inborn error of thyroid hormone synthesis (Option B) are structural or genetic issues unrelated to maternal autoimmune conditions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Iodine deficiency (Option D) is less likely in regions with adequate iodine intake and does not explain the autoimmune link in the mother's medical history."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 50-year-old woman presents with fatigue, weight gain, and changes in her skin appearance over the past year. On examination, her skin is noted to be dry with a yellowish tinge. There is puffiness around her eyes and non-pitting edema in her lower legs. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for her skin findings?","o":[{"t":"A) Increased dermal collagen deposition"},{"t":"B) Accumulation of dermal glycosaminoglycans"},{"t":"C) Hyperpigmentation due to adrenal insufficiency"},{"t":"D) Cutaneous manifestations of liver disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms, including fatigue , weight gain , dry skin , and non-pitting edema (myxedema) , points to hypothyroidism. In hypothyroidism, there is an accumulation of glycosaminoglycans (e.g., hyaluronic acid) in the dermis. This substance binds water, leading to non-pitting edema and the characteristic \"puffy\" appearance, particularly around the eyes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The yellowish tinge to the skin may be due to carotenemia, common in hypothyroidism due to reduced conversion of carotene to vitamin A."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The dry skin is a hallmark of decreased thyroid function, which reduces sebaceous and sweat gland activity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The non-pitting edema seen in this patient is due to glycosaminoglycan accumulation, which causes fluid retention but lacks the pitting typically seen in other forms of edema."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of Hypothyroidism:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased dermal collagen deposition (Option A): Glycosaminoglycans are the cause of skin changes in hypothyroidism and not collagen."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hyperpigmentation due to adrenal insufficiency (Option C): Pallor and yellow tinge are due to carotene accumulation, not hyperpigmentation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cutaneous manifestations of liver disease (Option D): While liver disease can cause yellowing of the skin (jaundice), the combination of symptoms and skin changes described is more consistent with hypothyroidism. The yellow tinge in this case is attributed to carotene accumulation, not bilirubin."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 25-year-old woman comes with complaints of lethargy and weight gain. Lab investigations are carried out which show the following results: TSH: 15 mIU/L (normal range: 0.4-4.0 mIU/L) Free T4: 8 pmol/L (normal range: 10-20 pmol/L) She has no known cardiovascular disease. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding this patient?","o":[{"t":"A) Start levothyroxine 25 mg daily"},{"t":"B) Start levothyroxine 100 mg daily"},{"t":"C) Start liothyronine (T3) 5 mg three times daily"},{"t":"D) Observe and repeat thyroid function tests in 3 months"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient has clear biochemical evidence of hypothyroidism with elevated TSH and low free T4 . Starting levothyroxine 100 mg daily is the most appropriate initial management for this patient with hypothyroidism who has no known cardiovascular disease."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hypothyroidism- Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Primary Treatment Levothyroxine (T4) replacement"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Initial Dosing - Young, otherwise healthy adults: 1.6 mg/kg/day"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(typically 100-150 mg daily) - Elderly or those with cardiovascular disease: Start with 25-50 mg daily and titrate up slowly - Pregnancy: May require 30-50% dose increase"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dose Adjustments - Based on TSH levels"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Adjust in 12.5-25 mg increments - Allow 6-8 weeks between dose changes for TSH to stabilize"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Monitoring - Check TSH 6-8 weeks after starting treatment or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"changing dose - Once stable, check TSH annually - Target TSH: 0.4-4.0 mIU/L for most adults"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Administration - Take on an empty stomach, 30-60 minutes before"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"breakfast - Avoid taking with calcium, iron supplements, or certain medications"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Special Considerations - Pregnancy: More frequent monitoring and dose"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"adjustments - Elderly: Start with lower doses and titrate slowly - Heart disease: Start with lower doses to avoid exacerbating cardiac symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Alternative Treatments - T3 (liothyronine) alone: Not recommended due to short"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"half-life - Combination T4/T3 therapy: Controversial, not routinely recommended"}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 78-year-old woman was brought to the emergency department by her family due to progressive lethargy over the past week. On examination, she is unresponsive with a core temperature of 32°C, BP of 80/50 mmHg, and HR of 40 bpm. Her skin is dry and cool, and she has periorbital edema. Initial lab results show: Sodium: 128 mEq/L (normal 135-145) TSH: 75 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0) Free T4: 3 pmol/L (normal 10-20) Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?","o":[{"t":"A) Oral levothyroxine 100 mg daily"},{"t":"B) Intravenous liothyronine 10 mg, followed by 10 mg every 8 hours"},{"t":"C) Oral levothyroxine 50 mg daily and oral liothyronine 5 mg twice daily"},{"t":"D) Supportive care only and repeat thyroid function tests in 24 hours"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This patient is presenting with myxedema coma (altered mental status, hypothermia, hypotension, bradycardia, and severe biochemical hypothyroidism) , a life-threatening complication of severe hypothyroidism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The correct initial management is to give Intravenous liothyronine (T3) 10 mg, followed by 10 mg every 8 hours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous T3 (liothyronine) is preferred initially because:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It has a faster onset of action compared to T4 (levothyroxine)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In severe hypothyroidism, peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 may be impaired"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Myxedema coma"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition Severe, life-threatening manifestation of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"decompensated hypothyroidism"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features - Altered mental status (from lethargy to coma)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Hypothermia - Hypotension - Bradycardia - Hyponatremia - Hypoventilation - Periorbital edema, macroglossia - Dry, cool skin"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Precipitating Factors - Infection"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Cold exposure - Trauma - Medications (sedatives, anesthetics) - Discontinuation of thyroid hormone"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnosis - Clinical presentation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Elevated TSH, low Free T4 - Associated lab abnormalities (hyponatremia, hypoglycemia, hypercapnia)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Initial Management 1. Intravenous T3 (liothyronine): 10-20 mg bolus, then 10"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"m g q8h 2. Intravenous levothyroxine: 300-500 mg loading dose 3. Hydrocortisone: 100 mg q8h (for potential adrenal insufficiency) 4. Supportive care: Mechanical ventilation if needed, cautious rewarming, fluid resuscitation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ongoing Management - Continue IV T3 until patient stabilizes"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Transition to oral levothyroxine when able to take oral medications - Identify and treat precipitating factors - Monitor for complications (e.g., cardiac arrhythmias)"}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 68-year-old man is admitted to the ICU with severe sepsis secondary to pneumonia. On day 3 of his admission, routine blood tests reveal the following thyroid function results: TSH: 0.2 mIU/L (normal range: 0.4-4.0 mIU/L) Free T4: 12 pmol/L (normal range: 10-20 pmol/L) Free T3: 2.1 pmol/L (normal range: 3.5-6.5 pmol/L) The patient has no history of thyroid disease. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?","o":[{"t":"A) Start levothyroxine replacement therapy"},{"t":"Start liothyronine (T3) replacement therapy"},{"t":"C) Start propylthiouracil to treat subclinical hyperthyroidism"},{"t":"D) No specific thyroid treatment; continue to manage underlying sepsis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's thyroid function tests show a pattern typical of sick euthyroid syndrome: Low T3 Normal T4 Low TSH"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low T3"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal T4"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low TSH"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This pattern is common in critically ill patients and is considered an adaptive response to severe illness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most appropriate management is to continue treating the underlying illness (sepsis) without specific thyroid treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low T3"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal T4"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low TSH"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sick Euthyroid Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definition Abnormal thyroid function tests in critically ill patients"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"without underlying thyroid disease"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Common Causes - Severe illness (e.g., sepsis, myocardial infarction)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Major surgery - Trauma - Starvation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Typical Laboratory - Low T3 (most common) Findings - Normal or low T4"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Normal or low TSH"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology - Decreased peripheral conversion of T4 to T3"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Increased reverse T3 (rT3)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Significance - Adaptive response to illness"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Severity correlates with prognosis - Resolves with recovery from the underlying illness"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Management - No specific thyroid treatment is required"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"- Focus on treating the underlying illness - Repeat thyroid function tests after recovery from acute illness"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Levothyroxine replacement is not indicated in sick euthyroid syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Liothyronine (T3) replacement is not recommended and may be harmful"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Propylthiouracil is used to treat hyperthyroidism, which is not present here"}]},{"n":15,"q":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for the management of Grave’s disease in the pregnancy?","o":[{"t":"Methimazole"},{"t":"Propylthiouracil"},{"t":"Radioactive iodine"},{"t":"Levothyroxine"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Propylthiouracil (PTU) is considered the drug of choice for managing Grave's disease during pregnancy, especially during the first trimester."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is because PTU has a lower risk of causing birth defects compared to methimazole and is preferred in early pregnancy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Methimazole (Option B) is effective for managing hyperthyroidism, but it is not preferred during pregnancy, particularly in the first trimester .It is associated with a higher risk of congenital malformations (such as aplasia cutis) if used during the early stages of pregnancy. It may be considered after the first trimester if necessary, but PTU remains the preferred choice throughout pregnancy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Radioactive iodine (Option C) is contraindicated during pregnancy due to its potential to damage the fetal thyroid and cause permanent hypothyroidism."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Levothyroxine (Option D) is used to treat hypothyroidism, not hyperthyroidism, in Grave's disease."}]},{"n":16,"q":"A pregnant female who is a known case of hypothyroidism is on levothyroxine. What are the levothyroxine dose changes recommended during pregnancy?","o":[{"t":"A) Decrease the dose of levothyroxine"},{"t":"B) Increase the dose of levothyroxine"},{"t":"C) Maintain the same dose of levothyroxine"},{"t":"D) Discontinue levothyroxine during pregnancy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"During pregnancy, the demand for thyroid hormones increases due to hormonal changes, particularly the increased levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which stimulates the thyroid."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Additionally, pregnancy increases the levels of thyroid-binding globulin (TBG), which decreases the amount of free thyroid hormones available."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As a result, women with hypothyroidism require an increase in levothyroxine dose to maintain normal thyroid function."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frequent monitoring of thyroid function is important to adjust the dose as needed throughout the pregnancy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hormonal changes during pregnancy"},{"k":"img","v":5214},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Discontinue levothyroxine during pregnancy (Option D):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Without proper thyroid hormone placement, the following complications are seen:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Maternal complications Neonatal complications"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Abortion Increased morbidity and mortality Preterm labor Cretinism Pre-eclampsia Neuropsychiatric illness Abruption Deafness and growth restriction PPH"}]}]);
QB.imgs({"5185":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5192":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5165":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5200":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5202":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5178":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5214":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});